# tools/al/ales_stereo_vibrato_witness.rish -- stereo_vibrato: the wet-only triangle-LFO modulated delay carried into stereo, the same LFO on both channels, each channel read off its own frozen snapshot -- the third rung of the stereo modulation class and the first that snapshots (ALES178).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES178.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-083330_fill-ales178-lotus-stereo-vibrato.md
# Stands on: lotus/vibrato.rye (ALES72 -- the mono wet-only vibrato run once per channel, its VibratoError reused whole and its precheck (newly factored out) reused for the up-front atomic validation), lotus/chorus.rye (ALES71 -- the
# full-wet chorus that is the dry plus this vibrato, the chorus-family equality target), lotus/glide.rye (ALES70 -- glide_scale, 256 = unity), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 -- the StereoClip), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 -- the Clip and the one true saturate).
#
# lotus/stereo_vibrato.rye wobbles a stereo master: it runs ALES72's proven mono wet-only vibrato on BOTH channels with the SAME LFO -- a triangle of depth round centre over period samples driving a wet-only modulated fractional
# delay, the delayed voice heard alone with no dry mixed under it. ALES176 (tremolo) and ALES177 (ring modulator) opened the stereo modulation class with the two thin per-sample modulations that write in place with no snapshot; the
# vibrato is the thinnest delay-line modulation and so the honest first delay-line stereo lift, the crux the snapshot discipline rather than the arithmetic. The same LFO on both channels, each on its own snapshot, is the linked stereo
# vibrato, both voices wobbling in lockstep. An LFO is scaled units the caller names (256 = unity), not scalars measured across the field, so the same LFO on each channel preserves the image for free; mono vibrato snapshots the dry
# prefix of its own clip, so run once per channel the left reads the left's dry and the right the right's, never crossed -- no cross-channel snapshot, the mono discipline twice. Because the interpolation read is a convex combination
# linear in the input, an identical-channel master stays identical and a panned master keeps its pan exactly, the left:right ratio untouched (the wet voice off the rail, so no saturation breaks it). It validates the LFO and span once
# against the shared length before either channel is snapshotted (BadPeriod, BadDepth, BadDelay, BadRange), so a refusal never wobbles one channel and leaves the other torn; VibratoError reused whole, no BadLevel (wet-only carries no
# mix). Values only, never a length; the two channels leave balanced. The crux is four laws: the stereo vibrato law (each channel equal to ALES72's mono vibrato with the same LFO byte for byte, the channels genuinely differing), the
# chorus-family law (a full-wet stereo chorus equal to the dry master plus this stereo vibrato per channel, sample for sample), the image law (an identical-channel master staying identical, a panned pair keeping its ratio), and the
# balance / silence / atomicity / degenerate law.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_stereo_vibrato_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-vibrato: Language EN — stereo_vibrato wobbles a stereo master by running ALES72's proven mono wet-only vibrato on both channels with the same LFO: a triangle of depth round centre over period samples driving a wet-only modulated fractional delay, the delayed voice heard alone with no dry mixed under it and no mix level. ALES176 the tremolo and ALES177 the ring modulator opened the stereo modulation class with the two thin per-sample modulations that write in place with no snapshot; the vibrato is the thinnest delay-line modulation and so the honest first delay-line stereo lift, the crux the snapshot discipline rather than the arithmetic, which is ALES72's reused verbatim. The same LFO on both channels, each on its own snapshot, is the linked stereo vibrato, both voices wobbling in lockstep. An LFO is scaled units the caller names, 256 being unity, not scalars measured across the field, so the same LFO on each channel preserves the image for free; mono vibrato snapshots the dry prefix of its own clip, so run once per channel the left reads the left's dry and the right the right's, never crossed, no cross-channel snapshot, the mono discipline twice. Because the interpolation read is a convex combination linear in the input, an identical-channel master stays identical and a panned master keeps its pan exactly, the left to right ratio untouched since the wet voice never touches the rail. It validates the LFO and span once against the shared length before either channel is snapshotted, so a refusal never wobbles one channel and leaves the other torn; it writes values only, never a length, so the two channels leave balanced."
say "ales-stereo-vibrato: Style Radiant — ALES176 and ALES177 opened the stereo modulation class with the two thin per-sample modulations, the tremolo and the ring modulator, both writing in place with no snapshot because every output depends only on its own input sample. The delay-line modulations are the class's other half, and the vibrato is the thinnest of them: the delayed voice heard alone, wet-only, no dry mixed under it and no mix level to name, so it carries only four faults and no BadLevel. That makes it the honest first delay-line stereo lift, the crux not the arithmetic, which reuses ALES72's driver and read verbatim, but the snapshot discipline carried cleanly into two channels. This rung runs ALES72's proven vibrato on each channel with the same LFO, so both voices wobble in lockstep and the image is held; each channel reads its own frozen snapshot, the left the left's dry and the right the right's, never crossed, so there is no cross-channel snapshot, only the mono discipline twice. Because the interpolation read is a convex combination linear in the input, an identical-channel master stays identical and a panned master keeps its pan exactly, both sides read at the same fractional delay off proportional snapshots so the left to right ratio at every sample is untouched, and the wet voice never touches the rail so no saturation breaks it. The crux is four laws: the stereo vibrato law, each channel equal to ALES72's mono vibrato with the same LFO byte for byte with the two channels genuinely differing so no channel is crossed; the chorus-family law, a full-wet stereo chorus equal to the dry master plus this stereo vibrato per channel sample for sample, so the wet-only lift sits under the eventual stereo chorus exactly as the mono rungs do; the image law, an identical-channel master staying identical and a panned pair keeping its left to right ratio at every sample; and the balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate law, an all-silent master wobbling to silence on both with the lengths held, a bad period, a bad depth, a bad delay top, and an out-of-range span each refusing by name with both channels untouched and balanced, and count zero the identity on both."
say "ales-stereo-vibrato: Lens TAME — two bounded in-process i16 clips (left, right), each rung through ALES72's own wet-only modulated delay, the triangle and the fractional interpolation run in i64 and each channel snapshotting its own dry prefix into one bounded max_clip-wide i16 scratch, the write saturating once as a proven no-op since the vibrato voice is a convex combination of two in-range dry neighbours and so already fits the i16 rail; the two channels enter balanced (the StereoClip invariant asserted on entry) and leave balanced (a vibrato writes values only, each length asserted equal and within bound on exit); the LFO and span are validated once against the shared length before either channel is snapshotted (ALES72's precheck, factored out of the mono vibrato and shared, mirroring the ALES75 ring modulator refactor), so a refusal never wobbles one channel and leaves the other torn; StereoVibratoError is ALES72's VibratoError (BadPeriod, BadDepth, BadDelay, BadRange) reused whole, the stereo lift adding no fault and there being no BadLevel since the vibrato is wet-only. The stereo vibrato law is proven by running both stereo_vibrato and ALES72's mono vibrato on the same per-channel content and demanding byte-for-byte equality on both channels while the two channels genuinely differ; the chorus-family law proves a full-wet stereo chorus equals the dry master plus this stereo vibrato per channel sample for sample against ALES71's mono chorus run per channel; the image law proves an identical-channel master stays identical and a right-is-half-left pan keeps its ratio sample for sample; and the balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate cases prove an all-silent master staying silent on both, the length held, each of the four refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, and an empty span the identity on both; siloed to lotus/, the LFO in scaled units rather than hertz, no cross-channel snapshot, no feedback, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_vibrato"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_vibrato.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-vibrato: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_vibrato.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-vibrato: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-vibrato: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-vibrato' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-vibrato: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-vibrato line"
